\cf2 "This is beautiful!" Chanta found himself caught up in the momentum, watching Elvi work with the efficiency of evolution, given jumpsuits and attitude. "It's like... like..."\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 "Like survival of the funkiest!" Tulsa completed, swerving around a Mercedes that had made the mistake of existing. "The One teaches that adaptation is holy, and brother, we're adapting the hell out of tonight!"\
By dawn, they'd hit seventeen mansions. The Lincoln convoy, now laden with guitar cases full of Gyges Discs, descended on the village of Mount Victoria like a sequinned avalanche.\
The Caf\'e9 Quantum occupied a corner that had been quaint before the Elvi arrived. Now it looked like quaint's nervous breakdown. White Lincolns filled every available space, including some that weren't technically spaces until the Elvi made them so.\
Inside, Johnny sat at what had been a quiet corner table, his latte growing cold as he watched Elvis after Elvis pile through the door. His carefully styled hair began to wilt from proximity to raw evolutionary energy.\
"Tony?" he called weakly.\
Chanta waved from behind a wall of pompadours. "Johnny! You made it!"\
"What... what is this?"\
"Success, brother!" Major Memphis cleared a path by the simple expedient of existing loudly. "The Great Teacher asked for discs, The Dawkins provided!"\
Johnny's mouth worked soundlessly as an Elvi thumped a guitar case onto his table. The latte jumped, committing suicide over the edge. The case opened to reveal Gyges Discs packed like temporal eggs in a reality-warping nest.\
"How... how many?"\
"This case? Bout twelve." The Elvi shrugged. "Maybe fifteen. Lost count after the place with the indoor waterfall."\
"Twelve?" Johnny's voice climbed octaves. "I asked for three!"\
"Three?" Another Elvi slammed down another case. "Brother, three's barely worth the gas!"\
Another case. Thump.\
Another. Thump.\
Another. Thump.\
Five cases on three tables, because the Elvi had run out of flat surfaces and started improvising. The caf\'e9 owner had given up and was now fixing breakfast for everyone, muttering about insurance.\
"This is..." Johnny stared at enough reality-warping technology to restructure existence. "This is too many!"\
"No such thing!" Colonel Kalamazoo opened the final case with a flourish. "The Dawkins teaches that excess is just evolution showing off!"\
Chanta leaned over to Johnny, who looked like he was calculating how many ways this could go wrong and running out of numbers. "So, about getting me into Sublime Point..."\
"I... you... this is..." Johnny picked up a disc, its surface reflecting his panic in fractal patterns. "Do you have any idea what this many discs could do?"\
"Make the universe more evolved?" suggested an Elvi.\
"End reality as we know it!"\
"Same thing, brother!"\
Johnny clutched his head. "I asked for three. Three! This is like asking for a calamari entree and getting a live kraken!"\
"Kraken's just an evolved menu item if you think about it," Major Memphis said philosophically.\
"That's not how evolution works!"\
"Says you."\
Chanta watched Johnny's face cycle through the stages of grief at locomotive speeds. "So... we have a deal?"\
Johnny looked at the guitar cases full of universe-ending potential, at the caf\'e9 full of Elvi discussing the theological implications of natural selection, at Chanta's expectant face.\
"I don't suppose you'd take some back?"\
"No returns, brother!" Tulsa McLean adjusted his rifle, which had somehow acquired more rhinestones during the night. "The Dawkins doesn't believe in backwards. Only forwards. With style. Evolve or die, brother.\'94\
Johnny carefully closed the nearest guitar case, his movements suggesting a man handling nuclear weapons while experiencing an existential crisis. "Fine. Take them. I don't want them. Store them... somewhere. Somewhere cool and dry and very, very far from me."\
"You don't want them?" Chanta blinked.\
"I want three. You can keep the rest. Consider them... evolution's dividend." Johnny stood, grabbed one case, thought better of it, put it down, grabbed a different one like it might be less catastrophic, then gave up. "I need to think. I need to drink. I need to call my insurance company and ask if they cover acts of Elvi."\
He grabbed three discs, shoving them into his jacket with the care of someone who's realised they're carrying pieces of the universe's self-destruct button.\
"Pleasure doing business!" Major Memphis called after him.\
Johnny fled, leaving behind guitar cases full of cosmic potential and a latte that had achieved enlightenment by osmosis.\
Chanta looked at the remaining cases. "So... what do we do with these?"\
"Store them somewhere cool and dry," Tulsa suggested. "The man was specific."\
"I know a place," said Suspicious Mick. "My cousin's got a storage unit. Keeps his jumpsuit collection there. Climate controlled and everything."\
And so the Elvi departed, leaving Mount Victoria forever changed and Johnny to wonder how he'd explain to Sarah that he'd asked for three discs and gotten more than enough to rewrite reality's operating system.\
Evolution, as it turned out, didn't do things by halves.
